1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,850
Sam had two scripts to write for Harlan: a fifteen-second hook and a thirty-second

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:05,838
founder explainer.

3
00:00:05,838 --> 00:00:10,283
He wrote the thirty first, since it had more to say, then took a red pen to it to

4
00:00:10,283 --> 00:00:11,216
make the fifteen.

5
00:00:11,216 --> 00:00:15,661
What came out read like exactly what it was: a rushed thirty-second script, not a

6
00:00:15,661 --> 00:00:16,978
real fifteen-second one.

7
00:00:16,978 --> 00:00:19,283
Video ad scripts fail for the same reason.

8
00:00:19,283 --> 00:00:23,674
A fifteen-second script and a thirty-second script aren't the same script at two

9
00:00:23,674 --> 00:00:24,113
lengths.

10
00:00:24,113 --> 00:00:28,558
They're different structures, built for different jobs, and the difference starts

11
00:00:28,558 --> 00:00:30,369
with how many words actually fit.

12
00:00:30,369 --> 00:00:32,125
How many words is that, exactly?

13
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:32,784
Not a guess.

14
00:00:32,784 --> 00:00:37,339
An actual number, worked out the same way the volume maths in Section 1.4 was: from

15
00:00:37,339 --> 00:00:40,577
the constraint, not from a feeling about what sounds right.

16
00:00:40,577 --> 00:00:45,077
Sam's mistake is the common one: write what you want to say, time it, find it runs

17
00:00:45,077 --> 00:00:47,601
long, then cut individual words until it fits.

18
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:52,156
That's editing under pressure, and it shows: a hook that used to land cleanly reads

19
00:00:52,156 --> 00:00:53,528
like it's missing a word.

20
00:00:53,528 --> 00:00:58,028
Write to the budget from the first sentence instead, and you never perform surgery

21
00:00:58,028 --> 00:01:00,882
on a script that was already trying to say too much.

22
00:01:01,432 --> 00:01:05,500
Here's the actual arithmetic, not a rule of thumb pulled from nowhere.

23
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,858
At a natural, unhurried spoken pace, fifteen seconds holds somewhere around

24
00:01:09,858 --> 00:01:11,427
thirty-five to forty words.

25
00:01:11,427 --> 00:01:13,519
Thirty seconds roughly doubles that.

26
00:01:13,519 --> 00:01:14,507
That's not a lot.

27
00:01:14,507 --> 00:01:17,936
Thirty-five words is barely two sentences of normal speech.

28
00:01:17,936 --> 00:01:22,817
A fifteen-second script has room for exactly one hook, one proof point, and one call

29
00:01:22,817 --> 00:01:26,071
to action, and nothing left over to handle an objection.

30
00:01:26,071 --> 00:01:30,313
Thirty seconds buys you the room to answer one hesitation before the CTA.

31
00:01:30,313 --> 00:01:32,173
Neither format has room for two.

32
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:36,996
This is the same discipline Section 2.3 taught for a single hook line, just applied

33
00:01:36,996 --> 00:01:39,437
to a whole script instead of one sentence.

34
00:01:39,437 --> 00:01:41,936
Specificity earned you a better hook there.

35
00:01:41,936 --> 00:01:46,643
A hard word budget earns you a script here that doesn't try to say everything and

36
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:48,444
ends up saying nothing clearly.

37
00:01:48,444 --> 00:01:51,233
Notice, too, that this budget isn't pessimistic.

38
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:55,069
Thirty-five words is plenty for one sharp idea, delivered plainly.

39
00:01:55,069 --> 00:01:59,834
The budget only feels tight if you're trying to fit a second idea in alongside it,

40
00:01:59,834 --> 00:02:04,076
which is exactly the instinct this whole lecture is asking you to resist.

41
00:02:04,626 --> 00:02:09,209
Here's that budget applied to a real script, using the raw testimonial hook from

42
00:02:09,209 --> 00:02:13,391
Section 2.3's matrix, the same one that ended up winning in Section zero.

43
00:02:13,391 --> 00:02:16,542
Four seconds to hook, seven to prove it, four to close.

44
00:02:16,542 --> 00:02:20,152
Thirty-one words, comfortably inside budget with room to spare.

45
00:02:20,152 --> 00:02:24,850
Notice what's not in here: no objection handling, no second proof point, no aside.

46
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:29,605
Fifteen seconds doesn't have room for a second idea, so this script doesn't pretend

47
00:02:29,605 --> 00:02:30,063
it does.

48
00:02:30,063 --> 00:02:34,818
Notice, too, that this script doesn't try to close every doubt a viewer might have.

49
00:02:34,818 --> 00:02:39,402
It closes the one doubt a fifteen-second cold-audience ad can actually afford to

50
00:02:39,402 --> 00:02:43,985
address, and leaves the rest for a warmer audience and a longer format to handle

51
00:02:43,985 --> 00:02:44,329
later.

52
00:02:44,329 --> 00:02:49,026
Read the proof line again: fifteen to twenty mmHg graduated compression, built for

53
00:02:49,026 --> 00:02:50,917
people who don't get to sit down.

54
00:02:50,917 --> 00:02:55,558
That's the exact spec from Harlan's product truth doc, the one Section 2.1 built,

55
00:02:55,558 --> 00:03:00,370
dropped into the script word for word rather than softened into something vaguer and

56
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:01,172
easier to say.

57
00:03:01,172 --> 00:03:05,755
A script this short has no room to paraphrase a claim and still land it clearly.

58
00:03:06,305 --> 00:03:10,445
The extra fifteen seconds buys you exactly one thing: room to answer a single

59
00:03:10,445 --> 00:03:12,595
hesitation before you ask for the click.

60
00:03:12,595 --> 00:03:16,949
That makes the thirty-second format the better fit for an audience further up the

61
00:03:16,949 --> 00:03:21,411
ladder from Section 2.2, someone who already half-believes the category and has one

62
00:03:21,411 --> 00:03:22,486
specific doubt left.

63
00:03:22,486 --> 00:03:25,712
A cold, unaware audience doesn't have a doubt yet to answer.

64
00:03:25,712 --> 00:03:29,959
They need the hook to work, full stop, which is exactly what the fifteen-second

65
00:03:29,959 --> 00:03:31,141
format is built to do.

66
00:03:31,141 --> 00:03:35,335
Picking the wrong format for the audience is a subtler mistake than it sounds.

67
00:03:35,335 --> 00:03:39,689
Give a cold audience a thirty-second script with an objection handled, and you've

68
00:03:39,689 --> 00:03:44,205
spent ten seconds answering a doubt they hadn't formed yet, seconds a fifteen-second

69
00:03:44,205 --> 00:03:47,699
hook would have spent earning their attention in the first place.

70
00:03:47,699 --> 00:03:51,838
Give a warm audience only fifteen seconds, and you never get to the one thing

71
00:03:51,838 --> 00:03:54,150
actually standing between them and a click.

72
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:55,924
The format isn't a budget choice.

73
00:03:55,924 --> 00:03:59,579
It's an audience choice that happens to come with a budget attached.

74
00:03:59,579 --> 00:04:03,773
Sam's rule of thumb: cold prospecting traffic gets the fifteen-second cut, and

75
00:04:03,773 --> 00:04:08,234
anyone already engaged, a previous ad watched, a site visit, gets the thirty-second

76
00:04:08,234 --> 00:04:08,665
version.

77
00:04:08,665 --> 00:04:13,019
Same product truth doc, two scripts, matched to how much convincing each audience

78
00:04:13,019 --> 00:04:13,664
still needs.

79
00:04:14,214 --> 00:04:17,477
One honest check before any of this goes near a generator.

80
00:04:17,477 --> 00:04:20,290
Word budgets are a useful estimate, not a promise.

81
00:04:20,290 --> 00:04:25,016
Delivery pace varies by person and by avatar, and a script that looks right on paper

82
00:04:25,016 --> 00:04:27,548
can still run long once it's actually spoken.

83
00:04:27,548 --> 00:04:31,823
Read every script aloud, with an actual timer, before you generate anything.

84
00:04:31,823 --> 00:04:36,549
It takes fifteen seconds to find out, which is a much cheaper fifteen seconds than a

85
00:04:36,549 --> 00:04:39,362
generated clip that needs trimming after the fact.

86
00:04:39,362 --> 00:04:43,919
Read it at the pace you actually want the avatar to speak, not rushed to make the

87
00:04:43,919 --> 00:04:44,594
number work.

88
00:04:44,594 --> 00:04:49,264
If it runs long at a natural pace, the fix is cutting a word, not asking the avatar

89
00:04:49,264 --> 00:04:50,108
to talk faster.

90
00:04:50,108 --> 00:04:53,258
There's a second reason to read it aloud, beyond timing.

91
00:04:53,258 --> 00:04:57,872
A script that scans fine on a page can still trip on a real mouth: a phrase that's

92
00:04:57,872 --> 00:05:02,204
easy to write and awkward to say, a word choice that reads clearly but sounds

93
00:05:02,204 --> 00:05:03,273
ambiguous out loud.

94
00:05:03,273 --> 00:05:07,830
You'll catch both of those by voice long before an avatar generator does, because

95
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:12,105
the generator has no opinion about whether a line is comfortable to deliver.

96
00:05:12,105 --> 00:05:15,706
It just performs whatever you gave it, clumsy phrasing included.

97
00:05:16,256 --> 00:05:20,741
Next lecture is where this script actually becomes a video: the avatar, properly

98
00:05:20,741 --> 00:05:23,600
licensed this time, performing exactly these words.

99
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,169
Before that, write your own.

100
00:05:25,169 --> 00:05:29,766
Pull an angle from the map you built in Section 2.4, one you haven't scripted yet,

101
00:05:29,766 --> 00:05:34,027
and write it to a fifteen-second budget: one hook, one proof point, one CTA,

102
00:05:34,027 --> 00:05:38,287
thirty-five to forty words, read aloud with a timer before you call it done.

103
00:05:38,287 --> 00:05:42,828
That script is what you'll actually generate on in the next lecture, and it's the

104
00:05:42,828 --> 00:05:47,369
first field in the B04 brief the workshop asks you to fill out four lectures from

105
00:05:47,369 --> 00:05:47,593
now.

106
00:05:47,593 --> 00:05:51,012
Notice how much of this lecture was really about subtraction.

107
00:05:51,012 --> 00:05:55,721
Every draft you write from here on will want a second proof point, a longer CTA, one

108
00:05:55,721 --> 00:05:57,123
more sentence of context.

109
00:05:57,123 --> 00:06:01,776
The word budget's whole job is to keep telling you no, so the version that actually

110
00:06:01,776 --> 00:06:06,372
ships is the one clean idea a viewer can hold in their head after fifteen seconds,

111
00:06:06,372 --> 00:06:08,447
not the crowded one you started with.
